---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import SiteCheckForm from "../../components/SiteCheckForm.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Inserts zero-width characters into your text", them: "Yes", us: "No" },
  { label: "Changes your text", them: "Yes", us: "No" },
  { label: "Survives cleaner tools, editing and reformatting", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Proves a stolen description was yours", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
  { label: "Produces evidence for a DMCA takedown", them: "No", us: "Yes" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Zero-width characters vs Kirpik"
  description="A plain comparison of the zero-width character trick with Kirpik: how the DIY fingerprint works, why it is detectable and strippable, and where proof of ownership comes from. Kirpik does not use zero-width characters."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Zero-width characters vs Kirpik"
    lead="A well-known DIY technique hides zero-width Unicode characters in text so that a pasted duplicate carries them along. The characters are invisible to readers but not to software."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What the zero-width trick is</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode includes characters with no visible width, designed for legitimate typography and text
        processing. The trick inserts them at chosen spots in a piece of text: the text looks
        unchanged, the pattern of insertions encodes a message, and if a duplicate turns up elsewhere
        with the pattern intact, you have a match. Variants of it have been used to trace leaked
        documents and to detect copy-paste plagiarism.
      </p>
      <p>
        The weakness is public. Zero-width characters are specific, known codepoints, so software can
        search for them by name. Code editors and their plugins highlight them. Security tooling flags
        them, because the same codepoints appear in phishing and obfuscation attacks. Online detector
        and cleaner tools remove them in one paste, and a find and replace across a handful of
        codepoints strips the rest. Many platforms normalise text on paste or save and drop the
        characters without anyone trying.
      </p>
      <p>Kirpik does not use zero-width characters.</p>

      <h2>Does it prevent content theft?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> Inserting invisible characters does not stop anyone taking the text: your
        content is still served through the machine-readable feeds (JSON, XML and ATOM) that scrapers
        actually read. What the trick adds is a fingerprint, and the fingerprint is detectable and
        strippable: it lasts only until the text meets an editor, a cleaner tool or a platform that
        normalises what is pasted into it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik does not claim to prevent content theft: it <strong>protects</strong> content. The
        watermark travels inside the text itself, so stolen content stays identifiable and provable
        wherever it surfaces. Kirpik adds no scripts to your storefront, so it runs alongside any
        blocker without conflict.
      </p>

      <h2>Can it prove infringement?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No.</strong> A surviving pattern shows that invisible characters are present, not whose
        text it was, unless the DIY encoding also carries an identity that can be decoded and explained
        under scrutiny. And it rarely survives, because the codepoints are removed by the tools and
        platforms described above.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik proves infringement through the watermark and the Proof of Ownership Certificate.
        Detection reads your watermark out of the stolen text, and the certificate records dated
        fingerprints, the infringing page's timestamps, Wayback Machine corroboration and a public
        verification page, ready for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="tablehead">Zero-width characters vs Kirpik</h2>
      <CompareTable themLabel="Zero-width trick" rows={rows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteCheckForm tone="band" heading="Is your store's content protected?" cta="Check my store">
    <Fragment slot="sub">
      Do you use <strong>zero-width characters</strong> or any other "Content Protection" App? Enter
      your Shopify store address to see if your content is actually protected.
    </Fragment>
  </SiteCheckForm>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <section class="fineprint">
        <p>
          This page describes the zero-width technique as publicly documented in editor plugins,
          security tooling and detector tools; behaviour varies with each implementation. It is a DIY
          technique rather than a product, so no app is quoted here. App-by-app comparisons are in
          <a href="/compare/">the full comparison survey</a>. Nothing here is legal advice.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablehead { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 1.15rem + 1.2vw, 2rem); }
  .fineprint { padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .fineprint p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 78ch; }
  .fineprint a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
